WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.575 --> 00:00:01.408
- Hello.

00:00:01.408 --> 00:00:04.195
Welcome to Ed talks with Khan Academy.

00:00:04.195 --> 00:00:08.201
I am excited today to
talk to Pedro De Bruyckeye

00:00:08.201 --> 00:00:10.711
and my apologies in
advance for not having the

00:00:10.711 --> 00:00:13.261
correct Belgian pronunciation of his name.

00:00:13.261 --> 00:00:17.266
He is an author who has
authored a number of books.

00:00:17.266 --> 00:00:21.203
We're gonna talk today about
urban myths about learning

00:00:21.203 --> 00:00:22.883
and education.

00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:26.901
And I'm excited to dive into
what we know from research

00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:30.535
that may disprove or suggest we are wrong

00:00:30.535 --> 00:00:33.751
about some of the things
that we hold to be true

00:00:33.751 --> 00:00:36.119
about learning and education.

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:39.401
I will remind you that Khan
Academy is a non-profit

00:00:39.401 --> 00:00:40.345
organization,

00:00:40.345 --> 00:00:43.983
and we are able to do our work
through generous donations

00:00:43.983 --> 00:00:45.423
from folks like yourself.

00:00:45.423 --> 00:00:48.894
If you go to khanacademy.org/donate,

00:00:48.894 --> 00:00:51.869
you will be able to contribute

00:00:51.869 --> 00:00:54.640
and help keep us moving
and working through

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.837
and doing all of the work that we do.

00:00:56.837 --> 00:00:59.636
So thank you for your
consideration of that.

00:00:59.636 --> 00:01:01.926
We also want to thank a
number of organizations

00:01:01.926 --> 00:01:05.820
who've stepped up during COVID-19
to help support our work,

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.270
including Bank of America,
AT&amp;T, Google.org, Novartis,

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.520
Fastly, and General Motors.

00:01:14.495 --> 00:01:15.328
A reminder,

00:01:15.328 --> 00:01:20.144
if you want to get an
audio version of this

00:01:20.144 --> 00:01:22.672
or any other Ed talk or a homeroom,

00:01:22.672 --> 00:01:26.741
and we have those available
in podcast version.

00:01:26.741 --> 00:01:28.527
Homeroom with Sal: the podcast,

00:01:28.527 --> 00:01:32.266
is available wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:01:32.266 --> 00:01:36.532
So with that, we're going
to talk today as I said,

00:01:36.532 --> 00:01:39.228
to the author of both of these books,

00:01:39.228 --> 00:01:41.163
Urban Myths About Learning and Education

00:01:41.163 --> 00:01:45.397
and More Urban Myths about
learning and education.

00:01:45.397 --> 00:01:47.836
Pedro, thanks for joining us.

00:01:47.836 --> 00:01:49.208
- Thank you for having me.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.041
Good.

00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:51.043
Hello.

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:52.268
- Hello!

00:01:52.268 --> 00:01:56.040
So the first question I like to ask folks

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.100
is to tell me a little bit
about how you got here.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.972
How did you end up writing
books about wording and

00:02:02.972 --> 00:02:03.805
education?

00:02:04.898 --> 00:02:06.856
- Well, it's, it's a funny story.

00:02:06.856 --> 00:02:11.523
I remember one evening, eight
years ago, nine years ago,

00:02:12.391 --> 00:02:16.120
and I was asked to be
the Statler and Waldorf

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.149
in a kind of education setting.

00:02:19.149 --> 00:02:23.174
And my only job was to give funny remarks.

00:02:23.174 --> 00:02:24.924
So I thought "Great."

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:30.878
And half way during the second
talk at a certain point,

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:33.420
one of the speakers was mentioning

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.359
that we have a creative brain
half and rational brain half,

00:02:37.359 --> 00:02:40.815
and I forgot that my mic was on.

00:02:40.815 --> 00:02:45.719
And I said, "Oh no, my bullshit
detector is now in the red."

00:02:45.719 --> 00:02:47.945
And everybody was watching,
(Kristen laughs)

00:02:47.945 --> 00:02:50.112
"What, what did you say?"

00:02:53.796 --> 00:02:55.126
And every time I still,

00:02:55.126 --> 00:02:57.966
when I'm meet that speaker,

00:02:57.966 --> 00:03:01.107
I still apologize for that moment.

00:03:01.107 --> 00:03:04.234
But he said to the audience,
I have to admit he's right.

00:03:04.234 --> 00:03:05.108
He's correct.

00:03:05.108 --> 00:03:05.985
(Pedro exhales)

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:06.868
But afterwards,

00:03:06.868 --> 00:03:09.968
a lot of people came over
to me and started asking,

00:03:09.968 --> 00:03:11.643
"but is this correct?

00:03:11.643 --> 00:03:12.896
Or what about this?"

00:03:12.896 --> 00:03:17.506
And I heard so many different myths

00:03:17.506 --> 00:03:20.338
and said somebody should
do something about this.

00:03:20.338 --> 00:03:22.891
And I was working my PhD,

00:03:22.891 --> 00:03:26.540
but I had a writer's block, I
have to admit, at that time.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.534
- And so,-
- Oh, been there, yes.

00:03:28.534 --> 00:03:31.395
- Yeah, we all have been there, I guess.

00:03:31.395 --> 00:03:32.290
And so I asked,

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.625
I asked my publisher,

00:03:33.625 --> 00:03:38.200
can I write a little small
book about urban myths

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.767
in education?

00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:42.152
And that was the starting point.

00:03:42.152 --> 00:03:45.275
Now the book has been
translated in English, Chinese,

00:03:45.275 --> 00:03:49.784
Turkish, Spanish, and it,

00:03:49.784 --> 00:03:51.954
and it has traveled the whole world.

00:03:51.954 --> 00:03:55.860
And I have traveled the whole
world talking about this.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.659
- And you managed to finish your PhD.

00:03:57.659 --> 00:03:59.668
That's really impressive.

00:03:59.668 --> 00:04:01.042
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:01.042 --> 00:04:05.313
I don't know how I've done
that, but I've done it, yes.

00:04:05.313 --> 00:04:06.146
- I understood.

00:04:06.146 --> 00:04:09.921
So how did you gather all
of these different myths?

00:04:09.921 --> 00:04:10.754
- Well,

00:04:10.754 --> 00:04:13.850
the first book was pretty easy

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:17.030
because we could look at
research with common myths

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:18.595
and common misunderstandings.

00:04:18.595 --> 00:04:23.595
And actually if we followed
the news just for one year,

00:04:23.659 --> 00:04:25.747
we already had a lot of them.

00:04:25.747 --> 00:04:27.440
And after the first book,

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.440
we thought, maybe a bit
naive, that we were done.

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:35.309
But when the first book was published,

00:04:35.309 --> 00:04:37.171
we received so many questions.

00:04:37.171 --> 00:04:38.610
"Yeah but what about this?

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.607
And what about that?

00:04:39.607 --> 00:04:40.945
And could you check this too?"

00:04:40.945 --> 00:04:45.156
And the second book is just
based on all questions received

00:04:45.156 --> 00:04:48.848
for the years after the
publication of the first book.

00:04:48.848 --> 00:04:49.713
- Wow!

00:04:49.713 --> 00:04:50.546
Nice.

00:04:50.546 --> 00:04:52.262
So that was that, yeah.

00:04:52.262 --> 00:04:55.501
Just raised all of these
questions for people.

00:04:55.501 --> 00:04:57.003
Just starting to hear about it,

00:04:57.003 --> 00:04:58.870
made them question other things.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:00.900
That seems like that's
a good outcome for the

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.270
first book to make people question more.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:05.316
- [Pedro] Yep, correct.

00:05:05.316 --> 00:05:08.438
- [Kristen] So let's dive
into a couple of them.

00:05:08.438 --> 00:05:11.578
We talk a lot here about
Khan Academy, at Khan Academy

00:05:11.578 --> 00:05:13.664
about the importance of knowledge,

00:05:13.664 --> 00:05:14.823
but there's this myth out there

00:05:14.823 --> 00:05:17.126
that if you can Google something,

00:05:17.126 --> 00:05:21.909
do you really need to learn
the knowledge piece of it?

00:05:21.909 --> 00:05:22.742
How about that one?

00:05:22.742 --> 00:05:24.666
What's your, what's your take?

00:05:24.666 --> 00:05:26.743
- Well, it sounds reasonable.

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:30.927
But the first question is how
can you come up with the right

00:05:30.927 --> 00:05:34.322
words to Google if you
don't know the subject,

00:05:34.322 --> 00:05:37.338
but the second element
and that's more painful,

00:05:37.338 --> 00:05:41.544
is that the algorithms
of, for instance, Google,

00:05:41.544 --> 00:05:46.524
they are not always
aiming at correct answers,

00:05:46.524 --> 00:05:51.076
but more aiming on what do
they think you want to know.

00:05:51.076 --> 00:05:54.360
And sometimes this can be incorrect.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:55.274
That's one thing,

00:05:55.274 --> 00:05:58.344
but also if you look at
information you are receiving

00:05:58.344 --> 00:06:00.618
and I'm not talking only about fake news

00:06:00.618 --> 00:06:01.717
and stuff like that,

00:06:01.717 --> 00:06:05.036
but you have a lot of people
who have the same name.

00:06:05.036 --> 00:06:09.375
And so we have had in the
past some very interesting

00:06:09.375 --> 00:06:12.262
discussions about famous artists,

00:06:12.262 --> 00:06:14.561
but then they were
discussing football player

00:06:14.561 --> 00:06:17.165
instead of the artists.
(Kristen chuckles)

00:06:17.165 --> 00:06:21.624
But there's an other very
important option, important reason

00:06:21.624 --> 00:06:24.710
because most of the
time you have to come up

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.904
with an answer straight away.

00:06:26.904 --> 00:06:30.221
You have to know things
to learn new things.

00:06:30.221 --> 00:06:31.886
If there's no prior knowledge,

00:06:31.886 --> 00:06:33.965
you have nothing to connect it to.

00:06:33.965 --> 00:06:37.223
And so you don't have the building blocks

00:06:37.223 --> 00:06:39.188
for the next learning step.

00:06:39.188 --> 00:06:40.488
And it's,

00:06:40.488 --> 00:06:43.453
it's a bit of a quote I once said,

00:06:43.453 --> 00:06:45.538
but I think it's sums it,

00:06:45.538 --> 00:06:47.031
it sums it up quite nicely,

00:06:47.031 --> 00:06:49.571
"On the internet you don't find knowledge,

00:06:49.571 --> 00:06:52.522
on the internet you find information,

00:06:52.522 --> 00:06:56.610
but what you need is the
knowledge to fact check

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.610
and to understand that information."

00:07:00.984 --> 00:07:03.170
- That all makes sense.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:06.209
And I like that knowledge
versus information,

00:07:06.209 --> 00:07:08.370
a distinction, that's really interesting.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:09.712
And I know the other piece is

00:07:09.712 --> 00:07:12.548
when you were solving
more complex problems,

00:07:12.548 --> 00:07:15.850
sometimes you need that base
knowledge to solve them.

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:18.953
And if you have to stop
every time to go Google

00:07:18.953 --> 00:07:20.417
for that base knowledge,

00:07:20.417 --> 00:07:22.697
you're gonna forget what you were doing,

00:07:22.697 --> 00:07:24.197
solving the complex problem

00:07:24.197 --> 00:07:28.063
while you're trying to find
an answer for the base one,

00:07:28.063 --> 00:07:30.396
is that right?

00:07:30.396 --> 00:07:33.361
- It's late in the evening over here.

00:07:33.361 --> 00:07:37.428
And one of my sons was just
passing through, going to bed.

00:07:37.428 --> 00:07:42.306
And actually I remember a
very nice example of this.

00:07:42.306 --> 00:07:43.139
A couple of years ago,

00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:46.409
he had to learn his tables
of multiplication by heart

00:07:46.409 --> 00:07:47.977
and he hated it!

00:07:47.977 --> 00:07:49.360
And he asked me, "Why do I need this?"

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:52.592
And then I went to a
door and I asked my son,

00:07:52.592 --> 00:07:53.664
can you open this door?

00:07:53.664 --> 00:07:54.756
Which he did.

00:07:54.756 --> 00:07:56.502
And I said "Now try to imagine,

00:07:56.502 --> 00:07:59.870
you can't remember how to open the door.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:03.557
So every time you are coming to the door,

00:08:03.557 --> 00:08:06.847
you have to try to open
it, try to figure it out,

00:08:06.847 --> 00:08:08.250
how you need to do this.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.690
You bang on the door, you
use a head for a second.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:11.913
Okay.

00:08:11.913 --> 00:08:13.509
If it's opened,

00:08:13.509 --> 00:08:15.028
you can only hope for one thing,

00:08:15.028 --> 00:08:16.644
that there is no second door,

00:08:16.644 --> 00:08:18.928
because otherwise you
will have to start over

00:08:18.928 --> 00:08:20.199
and over and over again.

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:23.130
Now you have to learn the
tables of multiplication

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:23.963
by heart.

00:08:23.963 --> 00:08:24.942
And it takes a lot of effort,

00:08:24.942 --> 00:08:27.977
but pretty soon you'll
need much more complex,

00:08:27.977 --> 00:08:31.981
you'll see much more complex mathematics.

00:08:31.981 --> 00:08:34.954
And if you need to calculate
your multiplications

00:08:34.954 --> 00:08:36.180
at that time,

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:39.039
you will lose so much time and energy,

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:42.822
so you won't be learning something new.

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:44.966
- That totally makes sense, yes.

00:08:44.966 --> 00:08:49.212
So I think that that importance
of knowledge for all,

00:08:49.212 --> 00:08:50.342
there's not just one reason.

00:08:50.342 --> 00:08:52.131
There's multiple reasons that,

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:54.859
that you've laid out here
on why it's important.

00:08:54.859 --> 00:08:55.859
Makes sense.

00:08:55.859 --> 00:08:56.692
All right.

00:08:56.692 --> 00:08:59.455
One of the most pervasive things,

00:08:59.455 --> 00:09:02.927
you talked about your detector going red,

00:09:02.927 --> 00:09:04.550
learning styles.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:08.133
That to me, it's so
common and people just,

00:09:08.996 --> 00:09:12.610
it seems so attractive for people

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:13.770
is something to believe

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:15.987
that there's visual learners
and auditory learners.

00:09:15.987 --> 00:09:17.684
And I hear it all the time.

00:09:17.684 --> 00:09:21.023
Tell me your thoughts on that one.

00:09:21.023 --> 00:09:21.856
- Well,

00:09:21.856 --> 00:09:25.955
it was one of the very first
myths that I encountered

00:09:25.955 --> 00:09:27.695
that put me on the right track.

00:09:27.695 --> 00:09:30.454
Actually, a couple of
weeks ago, months ago,

00:09:30.454 --> 00:09:32.119
Dan Willingham was on the show-

00:09:32.119 --> 00:09:32.952
- Ah, yes.

00:09:32.952 --> 00:09:33.785
Yes.

00:09:33.785 --> 00:09:35.140
- And it was one of his videos

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:37.658
that put me on track
of trying to figure out

00:09:37.658 --> 00:09:39.733
what about those learning styles.

00:09:39.733 --> 00:09:44.733
And what we've seen is a
lot of people recognize it.

00:09:44.837 --> 00:09:47.202
And first of all, it's true.

00:09:47.202 --> 00:09:51.173
A lot of people do have
learning preferences.

00:09:51.173 --> 00:09:53.464
Maybe some people like to read,

00:09:53.464 --> 00:09:55.722
or some people like to listen,

00:09:55.722 --> 00:09:57.088
but there is no of,

00:09:57.088 --> 00:09:58.686
no clear correlation

00:09:58.686 --> 00:10:03.256
between your learning
preference and better learning,

00:10:03.256 --> 00:10:06.990
but still that gut feeling,
"Oh, it's correct."

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:10.058
But I can flip that gut feeling.

00:10:10.058 --> 00:10:14.398
Just a question you have to
ask yourself as a teacher,

00:10:14.398 --> 00:10:18.773
are you in favor of
pigeonholing in education?

00:10:18.773 --> 00:10:21.815
No, because we think all
our pupils are different.

00:10:21.815 --> 00:10:23.370
All our students have different.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:26.413
Yeah but if you are using learning styles,

00:10:26.413 --> 00:10:30.267
you are pigeonholing your
students in three, four

00:10:30.267 --> 00:10:32.597
the maximum amount with NBTI,

00:10:32.597 --> 00:10:35.620
16 holes, 16 boxes

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:39.370
but our children are
much, much more complex.

00:10:41.068 --> 00:10:42.116
- Yes, yes.

00:10:42.116 --> 00:10:45.697
And it always is the,

00:10:45.697 --> 00:10:47.867
when you look at the research,

00:10:47.867 --> 00:10:49.858
there's just, no, it's,

00:10:49.858 --> 00:10:51.256
this isn't a case where,

00:10:51.256 --> 00:10:52.751
"Oh, the research hasn't been done."

00:10:52.751 --> 00:10:54.575
There's been lots and lots of studies.

00:10:54.575 --> 00:10:55.408
And they just,

00:10:55.408 --> 00:10:59.198
across all of them,
don't find this effect.

00:10:59.198 --> 00:11:01.343
And I wonder if there's still,

00:11:01.343 --> 00:11:04.478
if there's something here about research

00:11:04.478 --> 00:11:06.843
versus my personal experience

00:11:06.843 --> 00:11:09.176
that is causing this to be persistent

00:11:09.176 --> 00:11:12.194
and the idea of people
having a preference.

00:11:12.194 --> 00:11:17.194
- Well, there is something
else also happening in science,

00:11:17.616 --> 00:11:21.663
for instance, in a lot of
people write about education.

00:11:21.663 --> 00:11:22.819
And for instance,

00:11:22.819 --> 00:11:25.294
an area that there is a lot of research is

00:11:25.294 --> 00:11:27.393
on computers and learning.

00:11:27.393 --> 00:11:31.729
But those studies not often are
being done by psychologists.

00:11:31.729 --> 00:11:33.895
So what we've seen is a lot of research

00:11:33.895 --> 00:11:38.637
for the past 10, 20
years done on computers

00:11:38.637 --> 00:11:40.428
in education,

00:11:40.428 --> 00:11:43.162
but mentioning learning styles,

00:11:43.162 --> 00:11:46.023
not checking if this theory
is correct or incorrect.

00:11:46.023 --> 00:11:48.960
So you will find actually
a lot of research,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.613
not really on learning styles,

00:11:51.613 --> 00:11:53.584
but mentioning learning styles,

00:11:53.584 --> 00:11:56.122
feeding all the people
who think they believe

00:11:56.122 --> 00:11:57.786
"Yeah but I've read it in a study."

00:11:57.786 --> 00:12:01.353
Yeah but that study was
not done by psychologists

00:12:01.353 --> 00:12:02.653
knowing their stuff.

00:12:02.653 --> 00:12:04.600
They were done by computer scientists.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.564
They were done by
educationalists, perfect people,

00:12:07.564 --> 00:12:11.671
but they didn't know that
they were using something,

00:12:11.671 --> 00:12:14.300
they were using something incorrect.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.133
- Right, right.

00:12:15.133 --> 00:12:15.967
That makes sense.

00:12:15.967 --> 00:12:18.885
They almost assume that learning
styles was a thing before,

00:12:18.885 --> 00:12:21.112
as an assumption of the
work that they were,

00:12:21.112 --> 00:12:22.736
that they were doing, yeah.

00:12:22.736 --> 00:12:24.071
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:12:24.071 --> 00:12:27.821
So in that world of
computers and technology,

00:12:28.733 --> 00:12:31.433
also a space that I'm interested in,

00:12:31.433 --> 00:12:32.628
- No, really?

00:12:32.628 --> 00:12:33.587
- We hear a lot about dig-

00:12:33.587 --> 00:12:36.574
we hear a lot about digital natives and,

00:12:36.574 --> 00:12:39.197
but digital natives want
something really different

00:12:39.197 --> 00:12:41.705
with their education and their system.

00:12:41.705 --> 00:12:44.177
What is the research there on that?

00:12:44.177 --> 00:12:45.010
- Well,

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:48.564
digital natives first was
coined by Marc Prensky

00:12:48.564 --> 00:12:50.713
in 2001 already.

00:12:50.713 --> 00:12:55.135
And it was just based on
a couple of observations.

00:12:55.135 --> 00:12:57.146
It was not based on actual research.

00:12:57.146 --> 00:12:58.492
And again, it sounds logical

00:12:58.492 --> 00:13:01.654
because if you give a device to one of

00:13:01.654 --> 00:13:03.662
your children or your pupils or students,

00:13:03.662 --> 00:13:05.252
they are very handy with it.

00:13:05.252 --> 00:13:07.323
But there has been a lot of research

00:13:07.323 --> 00:13:09.122
and Paul Kirschner and myself,

00:13:09.122 --> 00:13:13.724
we've done a review on all the research

00:13:13.724 --> 00:13:15.342
since 2001.

00:13:15.342 --> 00:13:18.791
And what we've discovered
is that it's not true.

00:13:18.791 --> 00:13:19.720
It's not the case.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:24.045
You have to make a distinction
between the content side

00:13:24.045 --> 00:13:26.341
and the strategic side on one side.

00:13:26.341 --> 00:13:29.345
And well, the bottom
knowledge and it's true.

00:13:29.345 --> 00:13:31.741
Young people, young
students are much better

00:13:31.741 --> 00:13:35.537
in figuring out how
some device is working.

00:13:35.537 --> 00:13:39.194
But if we are talking about the content,

00:13:39.194 --> 00:13:40.994
but also how to use it,

00:13:40.994 --> 00:13:44.682
also to know if something
is correct or incorrect,

00:13:44.682 --> 00:13:48.999
then we see that they
are not that good at all.

00:13:48.999 --> 00:13:52.772
Actually, if we look
at 30 and 40 year old,

00:13:52.772 --> 00:13:55.918
they are much closer to
what has been described

00:13:55.918 --> 00:13:56.778
as digital natives,

00:13:56.778 --> 00:14:00.361
than 10 year old, 20 year olds.

00:14:03.113 --> 00:14:04.476
- Ah, interesting.

00:14:04.476 --> 00:14:07.383
I hadn't known that, that
piece of the last part.

00:14:07.383 --> 00:14:10.704
I remember looking, trying to
find in Marc Prensky's book,

00:14:10.704 --> 00:14:12.598
the research to support it,

00:14:12.598 --> 00:14:14.924
but he was basically coining a term,

00:14:14.924 --> 00:14:18.546
but without having necessarily
the resources to support it

00:14:18.546 --> 00:14:20.186
and it caught on, yeah.

00:14:20.186 --> 00:14:22.567
- And there was a lot of research

00:14:22.567 --> 00:14:23.400
and we've,

00:14:24.261 --> 00:14:25.333
we are,

00:14:25.333 --> 00:14:26.767
we keep checking all the research

00:14:26.767 --> 00:14:29.077
because maybe now it will change,

00:14:29.077 --> 00:14:31.526
but we didn't find any evidence yet.

00:14:31.526 --> 00:14:33.942
And in different different
regions, for instance,

00:14:33.942 --> 00:14:38.729
in, in China or Japan, we
can't find digital natives.

00:14:38.729 --> 00:14:40.066
We can't find them in Europe.

00:14:40.066 --> 00:14:41.928
We can't find them in the United States.

00:14:41.928 --> 00:14:44.009
So, sorry.

00:14:44.009 --> 00:14:46.817
Actually, it's a very important message

00:14:46.817 --> 00:14:50.744
because it's very important
for us as educators,

00:14:50.744 --> 00:14:51.577
as teachers,

00:14:51.577 --> 00:14:54.387
we need to train our students

00:14:54.387 --> 00:14:57.756
in how to handle these devices,

00:14:57.756 --> 00:14:59.242
how to work on the internet,

00:14:59.242 --> 00:15:01.932
how to handle the
information they are getting,

00:15:01.932 --> 00:15:05.999
but also to know when they
can use their cell phone

00:15:05.999 --> 00:15:08.415
and when it's better to put it away.

00:15:08.415 --> 00:15:12.456
I think that the fact that
there are no digital natives

00:15:12.456 --> 00:15:15.456
gives us an extra task in education.

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:18.959
- The idea of digital,

00:15:18.959 --> 00:15:22.630
digital literacy and what that means.

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.119
it's so important,

00:15:24.119 --> 00:15:27.202
but people have to be taught
it just like other skills.

00:15:27.202 --> 00:15:28.035
Yeah.

00:15:28.035 --> 00:15:28.868
- I guess so.

00:15:28.868 --> 00:15:29.701
- Yeah.

00:15:29.701 --> 00:15:33.300
We have a question from, from Facebook,

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:37.615
@malhafiz says "In today's
digital world we have knowledge,

00:15:37.615 --> 00:15:40.365
but how can we solve problems

00:15:41.403 --> 00:15:42.483
that many students,

00:15:42.483 --> 00:15:45.643
you know, have degrees, but
even then they get lost."

00:15:45.643 --> 00:15:49.263
And so I think that I'll ask
a related question to this.

00:15:49.263 --> 00:15:51.311
We've talked a lot about kind of myths,

00:15:51.311 --> 00:15:53.230
the things we haven't found,

00:15:53.230 --> 00:15:55.603
what does work for
learning, and particularly,

00:15:55.603 --> 00:15:59.032
maybe problem solving to
get at Mal's question.

00:15:59.032 --> 00:16:03.624
- Well, a lot of stuff works
in education, actually,

00:16:03.624 --> 00:16:07.441
not everything works and
nothing works all of the time,

00:16:07.441 --> 00:16:10.645
but there is a lot of things
we know that can do the job.

00:16:10.645 --> 00:16:15.289
And we know that basic knowledge

00:16:15.289 --> 00:16:19.887
and arithmetics and reading comprehension

00:16:19.887 --> 00:16:21.629
can help you a long way.

00:16:21.629 --> 00:16:25.021
But then there's also the
part of problem solving.

00:16:25.021 --> 00:16:27.834
We've seen for instance, from research,

00:16:27.834 --> 00:16:29.205
like project follow through,

00:16:29.205 --> 00:16:31.313
but also from research by the OCD,

00:16:31.313 --> 00:16:33.989
with the PISA results
and things like that,

00:16:33.989 --> 00:16:37.604
that base, a good solid basic knowledge

00:16:37.604 --> 00:16:42.554
can help you and also can
help you to solve problems.

00:16:42.554 --> 00:16:44.547
But then you get,

00:16:44.547 --> 00:16:48.083
you need the opportunity
to get in touch with this

00:16:48.083 --> 00:16:51.706
kind of problems and get the hang of it.

00:16:51.706 --> 00:16:53.517
So if you in education,

00:16:53.517 --> 00:16:55.936
if you have a good basic knowledge,

00:16:55.936 --> 00:16:59.737
if you have a lot background
knowledge, can help you too.

00:16:59.737 --> 00:17:02.911
But also you, if you
see a lot of examples,

00:17:02.911 --> 00:17:05.708
good examples of how problems are solved.

00:17:05.708 --> 00:17:07.155
If you get this,

00:17:07.155 --> 00:17:09.640
not only in one topic,
but in several topics.

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:11.739
Maybe in integrated topics,

00:17:11.739 --> 00:17:16.739
but then also give you
the opportunity to try

00:17:17.377 --> 00:17:18.272
and fail,

00:17:18.272 --> 00:17:21.268
but learn from the failures,
then you'll get a long way,

00:17:21.268 --> 00:17:25.756
just like you have to
learn how to read a book,

00:17:25.756 --> 00:17:27.816
how to write a sentence.

00:17:27.816 --> 00:17:32.149
It's also important to learn
how to solve a problem.

00:17:33.284 --> 00:17:35.879
- Yeah., so background knowledge,

00:17:35.879 --> 00:17:39.101
practice with feedback and whether

00:17:39.101 --> 00:17:40.737
- it's right or not.
- With feedback.

00:17:40.737 --> 00:17:42.346
- Yes.
(Kristen laughs)

00:17:42.346 --> 00:17:43.714
- And, and, and, you know,

00:17:43.714 --> 00:17:45.167
there's something that I want to add

00:17:45.167 --> 00:17:47.989
because when people are thinking that,

00:17:47.989 --> 00:17:50.879
when people know we are
writing about urban myths,

00:17:50.879 --> 00:17:54.279
they often think it's black or white,

00:17:54.279 --> 00:17:57.530
but we use three categories in our books.

00:17:57.530 --> 00:18:00.386
The first category is total nonsense.

00:18:00.386 --> 00:18:01.790
First, for instance, learning styles,

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:04.131
those are now total nonsense.

00:18:04.131 --> 00:18:08.582
If you're discussing some
other topics like multiple

00:18:08.582 --> 00:18:11.809
intelligences, then we say it's nuanced.

00:18:11.809 --> 00:18:15.918
And for some topics we
have to say, we don't know,

00:18:15.918 --> 00:18:19.469
because even in science, there
are a lot of discussions.

00:18:19.469 --> 00:18:22.236
Let's take the 21st century skills,

00:18:22.236 --> 00:18:24.281
it's a topic that's also very popular

00:18:24.281 --> 00:18:26.911
and closely related to problem solving.

00:18:26.911 --> 00:18:27.744
Now,

00:18:27.744 --> 00:18:31.512
there are two discussions
in the scientific world

00:18:31.512 --> 00:18:33.776
that we don't have a solution yet,

00:18:33.776 --> 00:18:36.270
but that are really relevant

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:38.933
for this kind of discussions in education.

00:18:38.933 --> 00:18:42.987
The first element we
are not sure about is,

00:18:42.987 --> 00:18:47.566
is it actually a skill

00:18:47.566 --> 00:18:50.197
or is it rather a personality trait?

00:18:50.197 --> 00:18:51.232
For instance creativity,

00:18:51.232 --> 00:18:53.903
is something that you can learn

00:18:53.903 --> 00:18:56.413
or is it something you need to develop?

00:18:56.413 --> 00:18:57.951
That's the first point of discussion.

00:18:57.951 --> 00:19:01.289
The second point of
discussion is how generic

00:19:01.289 --> 00:19:02.804
are those skills?

00:19:02.804 --> 00:19:03.718
Because for instance,

00:19:03.718 --> 00:19:07.461
I can be very creative
in playing the guitar,

00:19:07.461 --> 00:19:11.211
but it doesn't mean then I'm
very creative as for instance,

00:19:11.211 --> 00:19:13.334
a plumber, trust me, I'm not.

00:19:13.334 --> 00:19:15.586
So how generic are those skills.

00:19:15.586 --> 00:19:19.919
And we don't have the answer
on these questions yet,

00:19:21.811 --> 00:19:24.364
but I do think they are very important

00:19:24.364 --> 00:19:27.031
to help us further in education.

00:19:28.705 --> 00:19:32.911
- Yeah, so this idea that
research is evolving,

00:19:32.911 --> 00:19:34.690
I think, is an important one

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:38.018
and that we don't always
have all the answers,

00:19:38.018 --> 00:19:40.534
but psychology itself recently

00:19:40.534 --> 00:19:43.650
has been through quite a reckoning on

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:47.702
some things that we thought
were true after trying to

00:19:47.702 --> 00:19:49.731
replicate those findings,

00:19:49.731 --> 00:19:52.457
we're seeing aren't replicating so well.

00:19:52.457 --> 00:19:56.014
So do you see some, some
of that pattern too?

00:19:56.014 --> 00:19:56.847
- Yeah.

00:19:56.847 --> 00:19:59.498
Thank you for bringing this up, actually.

00:19:59.498 --> 00:20:04.101
Erik Erickson once described
that crisis means growth.

00:20:04.101 --> 00:20:05.399
Well, if that's the case,

00:20:05.399 --> 00:20:09.325
psychology has been through
a major period of growth,

00:20:09.325 --> 00:20:12.206
the biggest period of
growth in the history,

00:20:12.206 --> 00:20:13.392
because it's true.

00:20:13.392 --> 00:20:17.898
A lot of classic studies have
been replicated for the past

00:20:17.898 --> 00:20:21.242
10, 11 years and failed.

00:20:21.242 --> 00:20:25.516
And there was new new book
coming up next year, next summer,

00:20:25.516 --> 00:20:27.549
THe Psychology of Great Teaching

00:20:27.549 --> 00:20:29.690
and together with Casper and Liese,

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:31.224
what we have tried to do

00:20:31.224 --> 00:20:35.079
is trying to figure out
what is still standing

00:20:35.079 --> 00:20:38.037
and what should be left, left behind.

00:20:38.037 --> 00:20:40.966
And some of the big
theories, for instance,

00:20:40.966 --> 00:20:43.393
very popular in teacher training, Piaget,

00:20:43.393 --> 00:20:45.415
is one of the victims.

00:20:45.415 --> 00:20:47.439
But on the other side,

00:20:47.439 --> 00:20:50.923
there's also great news because yes,

00:20:50.923 --> 00:20:55.024
there's a lot of stuff that
wasn't able to being replicated,

00:20:55.024 --> 00:20:59.039
but there are also a lot of
things that are now standing

00:20:59.039 --> 00:21:00.210
stronger than ever.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:02.384
For instance, if we look at dual coding,

00:21:02.384 --> 00:21:06.392
a lot of cognitive science
that has been used in education

00:21:06.392 --> 00:21:08.485
for the past 10, 15 years,

00:21:08.485 --> 00:21:11.327
we have now a bigger knowledge confirming,

00:21:11.327 --> 00:21:14.180
scientific knowledge
confirming those insights,

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:15.525
- But,
- [Kristen] Great.

00:21:15.525 --> 00:21:20.296
- It has been, it has been
a very strange period.

00:21:20.296 --> 00:21:21.388
- It has, it has.

00:21:21.388 --> 00:21:24.257
So you're just kind of
dropped dual coding.

00:21:24.257 --> 00:21:26.266
Can you tell, talk to
folks about dual coding

00:21:26.266 --> 00:21:28.016
if they're not familiar with that?

00:21:28.016 --> 00:21:29.598
- Oh, sorry for that.

00:21:29.598 --> 00:21:31.662
Dual coding is the idea that

00:21:31.662 --> 00:21:34.206
if you combine images and words,

00:21:34.206 --> 00:21:36.467
we have two channels
in our working memory,

00:21:36.467 --> 00:21:39.359
a channel of for words,
a channel for images.

00:21:39.359 --> 00:21:40.752
If you use them both,

00:21:40.752 --> 00:21:43.880
there is a bigger chance
that will be learned.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:44.995
Okay, but wait,

00:21:44.995 --> 00:21:49.231
if you put too much pressure

00:21:49.231 --> 00:21:52.172
on one of both channels,

00:21:52.172 --> 00:21:54.339
then you are getting less learning.

00:21:54.339 --> 00:21:56.668
For instance, if there
are too much images,

00:21:56.668 --> 00:22:00.935
if the images don't relate
to what is being taught,

00:22:00.935 --> 00:22:02.472
people will learn less.

00:22:02.472 --> 00:22:06.157
But also if you have words on the screen

00:22:06.157 --> 00:22:10.802
that are exactly the same
of what is being said,

00:22:10.802 --> 00:22:12.832
then the word channel gets overloaded.

00:22:12.832 --> 00:22:15.832
And again, students will learn less.

00:22:17.952 --> 00:22:22.335
- That, that makes
sense that it's not just

00:22:22.335 --> 00:22:24.746
put all the information you
can through both channels,

00:22:24.746 --> 00:22:27.292
there's nuance to it
and how to do it well.

00:22:27.292 --> 00:22:28.500
And what that looks like.

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:29.333
- Yeah.

00:22:29.333 --> 00:22:32.807
I think that's the biggest
task we have as scientists.

00:22:32.807 --> 00:22:37.313
Not telling people "Do this
and miracles will happen."

00:22:37.313 --> 00:22:42.288
No, "Do this and maybe
this will have an effect.

00:22:42.288 --> 00:22:44.847
And if it doesn't work, please try this."

00:22:44.847 --> 00:22:49.138
because it's easy for gurus to say,

00:22:49.138 --> 00:22:51.388
"do this and everything will better

00:22:51.388 --> 00:22:53.453
and you will even smell better."

00:22:53.453 --> 00:22:54.286
But,

00:22:54.286 --> 00:22:55.504
(both laughing)

00:22:55.504 --> 00:22:56.831
in real life,

00:22:56.831 --> 00:22:59.788
it's much more complicated than that.

00:22:59.788 --> 00:23:01.073
Can I give you an example?

00:23:01.073 --> 00:23:02.325
- Yes, please.

00:23:02.325 --> 00:23:03.158
And it's,

00:23:03.158 --> 00:23:05.201
it's connecting several dots

00:23:05.201 --> 00:23:08.845
because we received a lot of
questions about growth mindset.

00:23:08.845 --> 00:23:10.659
And it's a topic in our second myth book,

00:23:10.659 --> 00:23:13.330
but also a topic in the psychology book.

00:23:13.330 --> 00:23:17.605
And Carol Dweck is not the guru.

00:23:17.605 --> 00:23:19.757
And I can prove you, she's not a guru.

00:23:19.757 --> 00:23:21.248
She's a real scientist

00:23:21.248 --> 00:23:24.154
because a lot of the
studies that were performed

00:23:24.154 --> 00:23:25.518
on a group mindset,

00:23:25.518 --> 00:23:29.955
had a very small group of
students being involved

00:23:29.955 --> 00:23:30.793
in the study.

00:23:30.793 --> 00:23:32.874
And for the past couple of years,

00:23:32.874 --> 00:23:35.791
there has been real that replication

00:23:35.791 --> 00:23:37.752
and credit to Carol Dweck,

00:23:37.752 --> 00:23:40.586
she also participated in research

00:23:40.586 --> 00:23:44.980
on replicating her original studies.

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:48.169
And we found out that the
effect of growth mindset on

00:23:48.169 --> 00:23:51.401
average is not that enormous.

00:23:51.401 --> 00:23:53.398
Well, it's really small.

00:23:53.398 --> 00:23:58.398
But the main words in my
previous sentence was on average

00:23:58.624 --> 00:24:02.388
because we see that for some
children, for some students,

00:24:02.388 --> 00:24:04.842
it can even have a negative effect

00:24:04.842 --> 00:24:08.138
to stimulate a growth mindset.

00:24:08.138 --> 00:24:12.445
But if the average is small
or nothing, close to nothing,

00:24:12.445 --> 00:24:15.317
and some pupils have a negative effect,

00:24:15.317 --> 00:24:18.422
then it's not hard to
imagine that other students,

00:24:18.422 --> 00:24:20.765
other pupils can have a positive effect.

00:24:20.765 --> 00:24:22.022
And we see that for instance,

00:24:22.022 --> 00:24:23.268
children from,

00:24:23.268 --> 00:24:27.822
younger children from
more poor backgrounds,

00:24:27.822 --> 00:24:31.867
they can benefit from a
growth mindset approach.

00:24:31.867 --> 00:24:33.011
So for us,

00:24:33.011 --> 00:24:35.979
it's very important not to
say throw growth mindset,

00:24:35.979 --> 00:24:37.751
throw growth mindset away,

00:24:37.751 --> 00:24:39.435
but when will we use it,

00:24:39.435 --> 00:24:41.262
for who will we use it,

00:24:41.262 --> 00:24:43.788
but also keep an eye on the effects

00:24:43.788 --> 00:24:45.553
because maybe in your case,

00:24:45.553 --> 00:24:48.386
it's gonna have a
perfect, splendid effect,

00:24:48.386 --> 00:24:50.258
but maybe it's the opposite.

00:24:50.258 --> 00:24:53.195
And then it's you as a teacher or educator

00:24:53.195 --> 00:24:56.945
or as a team to say,
let's do something else.

00:24:58.304 --> 00:25:00.317
- Yes, so Sarah,

00:25:00.317 --> 00:25:02.732
Sarah Hem from YouTube, you asks if,

00:25:02.732 --> 00:25:06.015
if Pedro could say something
about motivation myths.

00:25:06.015 --> 00:25:10.525
So there's a piece about a mindset,

00:25:10.525 --> 00:25:11.358
a mindset,

00:25:11.358 --> 00:25:14.236
and how think about motivation
from growth mindset.

00:25:14.236 --> 00:25:16.643
That's I think important.

00:25:16.643 --> 00:25:19.250
Anything else on motivation?

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:20.083
- Oh,

00:25:20.083 --> 00:25:22.304
I can tell a lot of them about motivation

00:25:22.304 --> 00:25:25.702
because there's a lot
about it in the new book,

00:25:25.702 --> 00:25:29.422
but I give you two very
interesting insights.

00:25:29.422 --> 00:25:32.734
The first thing we often
think about you have,

00:25:32.734 --> 00:25:36.504
or intrinsic motivation
or extrinsic motivation

00:25:36.504 --> 00:25:39.907
and intrinsic is good
and extrinsic is bad,

00:25:39.907 --> 00:25:42.990
but it's much, much more complicated.

00:25:44.202 --> 00:25:45.035
For instance,

00:25:45.035 --> 00:25:47.982
there are a couple of versions
of extrinsic motivation,

00:25:47.982 --> 00:25:52.972
and I can give you again,
a very simple example.

00:25:52.972 --> 00:25:56.882
You have a student who
wants to become a doctor.

00:25:56.882 --> 00:26:00.633
He or she is really
motivated to become a doctor,

00:26:00.633 --> 00:26:04.263
but he or she is not
really into mathematics,

00:26:04.263 --> 00:26:07.059
but they noticed that student knows that

00:26:07.059 --> 00:26:09.921
if I want to become a doctor,

00:26:09.921 --> 00:26:13.416
I really need this kind of mathematics.

00:26:13.416 --> 00:26:14.964
Then that student,

00:26:14.964 --> 00:26:18.292
isn't motivated intrinsically
for mathematics,

00:26:18.292 --> 00:26:22.109
but has a good, positive
extrinsic motivation.

00:26:22.109 --> 00:26:24.644
Please keep that kind of motivation.

00:26:24.644 --> 00:26:25.477
But also,

00:26:25.477 --> 00:26:27.781
and this will surprise a lot of you.

00:26:27.781 --> 00:26:30.748
We know that a lot of people
think the more passionate you

00:26:30.748 --> 00:26:33.066
are about the subject, the better,

00:26:33.066 --> 00:26:37.306
but there is an issue with that

00:26:37.306 --> 00:26:38.139
because we know

00:26:38.139 --> 00:26:41.081
that if you're really, really
passionate about something,

00:26:41.081 --> 00:26:43.971
but you don't have
anything else in your life,

00:26:43.971 --> 00:26:47.859
then there is this bigger
chance that when you have,

00:26:47.859 --> 00:26:51.048
whenever you get too
into a problem or hurdle

00:26:51.048 --> 00:26:53.175
that you will give up.

00:26:53.175 --> 00:26:54.323
if you have a passion,

00:26:54.323 --> 00:26:57.929
but you also have something
on the side, something else,

00:26:57.929 --> 00:26:59.584
another hobby,

00:26:59.584 --> 00:27:03.985
then you are more resilient
for any kind of setbacks

00:27:03.985 --> 00:27:07.804
you can encounter trying
to achieve your passion.

00:27:07.804 --> 00:27:12.339
So the idea of being more
motivated or more passionate

00:27:12.339 --> 00:27:13.172
is better.

00:27:13.172 --> 00:27:17.043
It's again, much more
complicated than that.

00:27:17.043 --> 00:27:18.021
- Yes.

00:27:18.021 --> 00:27:20.597
Okay, So this relates to a
question from Alex Marelli,

00:27:20.597 --> 00:27:21.809
from YouTube, who says,

00:27:21.809 --> 00:27:24.157
"You mentioned your son
not liking to memorize his

00:27:24.157 --> 00:27:25.337
multiplication tables.

00:27:25.337 --> 00:27:28.649
How important is student
drive in learning?"

00:27:28.649 --> 00:27:30.646
And I think there's a followup question of

00:27:30.646 --> 00:27:33.138
if a student's not
motivated to do something,

00:27:33.138 --> 00:27:37.896
how do you try to get them to
be motivated to do something?

00:27:37.896 --> 00:27:38.788
- Yeah, yeah.

00:27:38.788 --> 00:27:40.626
That's the $1 million question

00:27:40.626 --> 00:27:43.090
because we all, first of all,

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:46.247
I think we have to accept
even us as a teacher.

00:27:46.247 --> 00:27:50.129
We're not always that
motivated for every single part

00:27:50.129 --> 00:27:51.908
of the things we are doing.

00:27:51.908 --> 00:27:52.741
So that's.

00:27:52.741 --> 00:27:54.420
- Sometimes I treat myself with chocolate

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:56.857
to get through a task
that I have to do, I know.

00:27:56.857 --> 00:27:58.321
- You should come to Belgium,

00:27:58.321 --> 00:28:00.738
the best chocolates in the world.

00:28:00.738 --> 00:28:01.986
I wasn't paid for that, okay.

00:28:01.986 --> 00:28:04.084
(both laughing)

00:28:04.084 --> 00:28:06.667
But going back to the question,

00:28:08.564 --> 00:28:10.959
one of the things is we to find

00:28:10.959 --> 00:28:14.042
that they experience kind of success.

00:28:15.572 --> 00:28:19.040
So if it's too difficult or too easy,

00:28:19.040 --> 00:28:23.373
I think a first step is
make sure that everything is

00:28:25.643 --> 00:28:26.811
a possible challenge.

00:28:26.811 --> 00:28:28.043
It's a challenge,

00:28:28.043 --> 00:28:31.234
but a challenge that they can achieve,

00:28:31.234 --> 00:28:33.828
you know, the, the classic
concept of scaffolding,

00:28:33.828 --> 00:28:38.104
good teaching is building scaffolds to,

00:28:38.104 --> 00:28:40.596
for every child to learn.

00:28:40.596 --> 00:28:43.798
Sometimes you will need a kind of rewards

00:28:43.798 --> 00:28:45.083
to get things started,

00:28:45.083 --> 00:28:48.542
but there another element
and that's often overlooked.

00:28:48.542 --> 00:28:50.233
We've all had teachers,

00:28:50.233 --> 00:28:54.271
that teaches on subjects that
we weren't interested in.

00:28:54.271 --> 00:28:58.495
But surprisingly we were
by the end of the class

00:28:58.495 --> 00:28:59.328
because the teacher,

00:28:59.328 --> 00:29:02.487
he or himself was so passionate about

00:29:02.487 --> 00:29:05.672
and passion can be very contagious.

00:29:05.672 --> 00:29:06.505
So,

00:29:07.424 --> 00:29:10.536
try to be motivated yourself, showed us,

00:29:10.536 --> 00:29:14.512
try to explain why you're motivated,

00:29:14.512 --> 00:29:17.550
but also try to explain
why the child needs this.

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:21.371
Because I have to admit,
discussing my son again,

00:29:21.371 --> 00:29:24.336
as an example, I don't like anecdotes,

00:29:24.336 --> 00:29:27.369
but I want to use this
to explain something,

00:29:27.369 --> 00:29:31.582
is that well, I didn't convince him,

00:29:31.582 --> 00:29:33.174
but he did it because he thought,

00:29:33.174 --> 00:29:34.964
"Well, if my dad thinks it's important,

00:29:34.964 --> 00:29:36.525
it will probably be important."

00:29:36.525 --> 00:29:37.993
But later on,

00:29:37.993 --> 00:29:40.018
he came to me and said, "You were right.

00:29:40.018 --> 00:29:41.415
I need this."

00:29:41.415 --> 00:29:45.406
And sometimes you have to
ask a kind of leap of faith

00:29:45.406 --> 00:29:47.206
from your student or children.

00:29:47.206 --> 00:29:49.440
We know you, I know it's hard now,

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:51.664
but you'll get by.

00:29:51.664 --> 00:29:55.122
And then you'll figure
out why you need this.

00:29:55.122 --> 00:29:57.881
But one last remark on this.

00:29:57.881 --> 00:30:01.705
If you don't know why do you need this,

00:30:01.705 --> 00:30:04.025
why your students need this,

00:30:04.025 --> 00:30:06.766
and the only answer is it's
because it's in the textbook

00:30:06.766 --> 00:30:09.698
or in the curriculum, then
you're in deep trouble.

00:30:09.698 --> 00:30:11.692
Because if you can't answer that question,

00:30:11.692 --> 00:30:14.609
how can you motivate your children?

00:30:17.558 --> 00:30:19.926
- That, that totally makes sense.

00:30:19.926 --> 00:30:22.068
It sounds like a good
place to leave us on.

00:30:22.068 --> 00:30:23.987
I could have talked to
you for much longer,

00:30:23.987 --> 00:30:26.620
but we are already at the
end of our time today.

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:28.885
Thank you so much for joining us.

00:30:28.885 --> 00:30:30.791
- Oh, it was great fun, thank you.

00:30:30.791 --> 00:30:31.624
- Excellent.

00:30:31.624 --> 00:30:34.306
And I want to, again,
recommend to everyone,

00:30:34.306 --> 00:30:38.257
both the existing books on
urban myths in learning and

00:30:38.257 --> 00:30:39.090
education.

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:43.400
And we look forward to your
forthcoming book as well.

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:46.817
Thank you and everyone have a great day.

